## Tokens, not values

All visual styling comes from the semantic CSS variables in `design/tokens.css`. Hex, rgb, hsl,
and px spacing values in prototype code are errors, not shortcuts.

{{TOKENS: Document token groups with their naming patterns and usage rules (which color is used where, spacing tiers, typography tiers with line heights, radius language, shadows). Keep it brief—the file itself is the reference. For each role, include the REASON in half a sentence (for example, “Use shadows only on the header because the system otherwise separates content with surfaces”) so agents can extrapolate missing patterns consistently instead of drifting. Explicitly state the role of the brand color, the text hierarchy (primary/secondary/soft), and the surface tiers. ALWAYS specify which surface/color indicates selection or an active state (selected option, active filter, active navigation), and which similar color is NOT intended for that purpose. Without this sentence, every prototype agent reaches for the nearest pastel surface. Declare categories the DS deliberately DOES NOT have (for example, no shadows, animations, or gradients) as “Intentionally absent: …—reason” instead of silently omitting them; otherwise the next agent will invent them.}}

Widths from the CSS framework's utility scale (`max-w-*`, `min-w-*`) are allowed as long as
they are rem-based and no token equivalent exists; they constrain content within a section.
All colors, spacing, and typography must still come exclusively from tokens.

## Choosing components

Native elements are almost always the wrong answer. `<button>` → `<DsButton>`, `<input>` →
the form components in this kit. Before building anything custom, check `components-meta.json`—
{{ANZAHL}} components exist.

{{KOMPONENTENWAHL: Provide a table mapping native element/intent → kit component, including selection patterns (radio/checkbox/option tile). Then add “Typical compositions”: 5–10 recurring patterns (data page, form, card/teaser, empty state, confirmation dialog, and so on), each with the specific component combination used in this kit and described in 1–3 lines. Always include the TABLE/DATA PATTERN—line color, header weight, alignment, number formatting (tabular-nums), horizontal scrolling—because otherwise every agent invents its own table design.}}

For image placeholders smaller than roughly 120px on either side (list rows, table headers,
thumbnails), text no longer fits on the surface. Center an icon there and provide the subject
description as `sr-only` text—clipped placeholder text looks like an error, not a placeholder.

